WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.724
Module seven builds the engine of the twin: a traffic simulation that is calibrated and validated well enough to be trusted.

2
00:00:07.624 --> 00:00:09.304
First choose the zoom level.

3
00:00:09.304 --> 00:00:14.104
Macroscopic and mesoscopic models move flows or platoons and suit whole regions.

4
00:00:14.104 --> 00:00:21.004
Microscopic models simulate every driver and suit corridors, junctions and signals, at the price of speed and data.

5
00:00:21.904 --> 00:00:27.071
Inside a microscopic model, car-following rules decide how drivers keep their distance.

6
00:00:27.071 --> 00:00:30.516
SUMO's default is the Krauss model, Vissim uses Wiedemann.

7
00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:33.604
Lane-changing and route choice complete the picture.

8
00:00:34.504 --> 00:00:45.784
In SUMO the workflow is: build the network with netconvert, generate demand and routes, add detectors, run sumo or sumo-gui, and read the outputs such as trip information and detector counts.

9
00:00:46.684 --> 00:00:50.876
Calibration adjusts demand and parameters until the model matches counts.

10
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:55.642
A common target is GEH below five on at least eighty-five percent of counted links.

11
00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:59.088
Then validate on different days, and check travel times too.

12
00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:02.018
Finally, one run is not a result.

13
00:01:02.018 --> 00:01:03.433
Simulations are random.

14
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:09.032
Run several seeds, report the mean and the range, and never tune and test on the same seed.

15
00:01:09.032 --> 00:01:12.354
In the SUMO lab you will see what happens when you do.
